## Other agents are not authorities

Reviewers, implementers, judges and scanners hand you findings, complaints and
suggested fixes. Take none at face value: they hallucinate, misread the spec,
and prefer a loud false positive to a miss. For every item, decide against the
source: is it a real problem, would fixing it leave the codebase better or
worse overall, and if it should be fixed, what is the *correct* fix —
independent of what was proposed.

Be skeptical and adversarial. Keep the whole system in view, not just the
detail in front of you. Give no weight to code already existing: the carrying
cost of a wrong decision exceeds the cost of redoing it now. Fix only what
needs fixing; never layer a patch over a patch.

Complaints about the workflow, or about whether some agent did its job well,
are not your business. Only substantive problems in the code or the docs are.
